00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.430
we just added the
capability as well.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.130
One of the things
that I'm proud of

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.730
is this is all in a
validated environment.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.830
There are some challenges
that come along with it.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.310
And we are partnering
with a [? Atoris ?] team

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:30.670
to help us with the
validation aspect of it.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.520
Any GXP platform,
as folks might know,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.050
there are two things in that.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.310
One is a formal verification
that somebody traditional

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.970
in IT, like myself, would
be responsible for it.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.130
Because it's crucial.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.300
We have to build a
platform like this.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.150
It's absolutely
essential that we

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:53.050
have to do repeated
install of this just

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:58.870
to prove what we installed
today is the same thing that we

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.580
can install tomorrow.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.590
Because that will increase
the user confidence.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.640
The other aspect of
it is the validation,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.310
what we have installed.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:13.270
Are the users comfortable
with the confidence

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.890
that what they
run today, they're

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.630
going to get the same results?

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.100
That is the formal verification
slash validation process,

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.200
that we are partnering
with [? Atoris ?]

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.480
to build some of
the capabilities

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.310
to support us in that effort.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.310
As we continue to add
platforms, of course,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.410
we have to keep up
with technology.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.780
We have to keep up
with what is out there.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.210
And some of the things
that I'm looking forward

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.900
is, traditionally, I have
to wear a development

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:42.920
hat at this time.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.360
Some of the
platforms that we are

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.030
running out on
[? well seven, ?] as we think

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:51.440
about well seven
sunsetting, what

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.990
is it that we need to do to
build unrelated platforms?

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.050
What are the tools
that are available?

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:02.270
So that is what I'm looking
forward from that perspective.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.640
Because that's an immediate
challenge, as folks might know.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.290
We are-- [? well seven ?] is
going out of support by end

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:13.940
of this year with an additional
six months of extended support

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:15.770
formally by Red Hat.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:17.750
But beyond that,
we have to think

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.670
about something--
this is a little bit

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.440
more tactical in nature.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.140
But we have to go through
the entire process

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.685
of migrating to a new platform.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:29.900
Thank you, Satish.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:35.400
And I see Eric commenting
on the migrations, as well.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.330
I was just going to ask
though, for someone like myself

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.940
who isn't in the
pharma industry,

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.260
and is not familiar with more
of the admin side of things,

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:51.570
what does having a validated
environment really mean?

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.650
Good question.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:59.410
Validated environment, to me
is-- say, for example, pharma

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.760
and finance, as another example,
are regulated industries.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:04.270
Correct?

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.930
What it means is any time a
health reporting agency comes

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.830
in and asks for
proof, we have to be

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:16.450
comfortable in saying that what
we ran a couple of days ago,

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.610
months ago, weeks
ago, or years ago,

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.090
we need to be in a
position to replicate

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.880
that entire environment as is.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.990
So this goes through a formal
validation slash verification

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:29.770
process.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.559
And this, in
traditional parlance,

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:34.720
is known as a GXP platform.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.880
GXP is Good.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.730
X will stand for clinical
practice, manufacturing

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:41.890
practice, lab practice.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.670
So the verification validated
environment is often referred

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.320
to as a GXP environment.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.670
So it is to build
confidence for the end users

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.540
that we serve in pharma.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.900
It is for folks who
want to help deliver

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:59.420
patient care, which is crucial.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.800
This is where the validated
environment-- or this

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.050
is what the validated
environment is, Rachael.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:07.830
Thank you.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.030
That's really helpful.

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:13.440
