00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.500
But somebody had asked,
when talking about R,

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.260
we hear a lot from a data
science or engineering

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:23.400
perspective.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.040
What do you think the
differences in thought

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.180
are from an IT perspective?

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:31.740
From an IT perspective.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.160
Good question.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:37.410
So the one advantage
that we have with R

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.620
is, as I mentioned earlier,
the power of open source,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.070
where folks are contributing
to the R ecosystem as such.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:49.080
So there are more and more folks
who are contributing to it.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.160
It could be, at
the end of the day,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.720
it's a traditional R
package that gets developed.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.760
And often, what we are seeing
is, from an IT perspective,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.120
there are some challenges
in reproducing this platform

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:04.620
as is.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:08.130
Sometimes the frustration
that users have is,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.955
hey, it worked
yesterday, why am I--

00:10:11.955 --> 00:10:14.260
I tried the exact
same thing today.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.890
Why am I having issues with it?

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.310
There are various reasons.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.310
That's because, you
know, an underlying-- you

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:24.400
might be using version one
of one particular package.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.610
But underneath that,
it will go and pull

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:30.510
certain dependencies, which
could be a function of time.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.290
But there are tools
that are available

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:38.410
today to help us
with that approach.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.530
In other words,
there is an R package

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:43.020
that folks are familiar
with, R, E, and B,

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.810
as an example, where you
can lock the versions.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:49.260
We are also using Deposit
Package Manager as well,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.290
to help us with
snapshotting capabilities.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:57.840
And this is where IT can step
in, implement these tools,

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.420
so that users can be confident
that what they did today

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.960
they should be able to reproduce
the same environment tomorrow,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.950
as well.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.270
So Bill had asked, do
you have SaaS and R running

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.310
on the same validated server?

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:17.560
Is that server in the cloud?

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:22.680
And is that managed by an
external service provider?

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.630
Maybe I'll try and break this
up into multiple questions.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:28.290
But Bill also asks, are
you having a hard time

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:32.430
finding a cloud hosting
service provider?

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.830
Who is doing that for you today?

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.960
Fair question.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.460
One, we have completely
adopted a cloud, at least

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:42.600
inside Janssen.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.140
Most of what we do, it is
with the cloud provider.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:49.200
And we have a huge
investment with AWS.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.810
We are partnering
with AWS, as and when

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:56.220
we would want new capabilities,
we do work with them

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:01.110
and see where they could help
us implement certain services

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.460
that we would need.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.290
Now AWS might be ready, but then
our technology services team

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:10.230
would want to ensure,
because it's in the cloud,

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.740
that there are as several
security policies in place.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.890
So we do have to go
through them to ensure

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:21.210
that the services offered by
any cloud service provider

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:24.130
is blessed by our security team.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.850
That's how we work.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:30.960
Number two, I think
the question was--

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:31.830
yeah, right.

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:35.520
So we run everything in AWS.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:39.900
We do-- I don't want to call it
"a" server hosting SaaS and R.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:43.200
Because end of the day,
from a tactical perspective,

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.300
these are different
assets, but it

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:49.260
is a part of the same ecosystem,
as I mentioned earlier.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:54.270
That is the statistical and
program compute environment

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.850
which is a validated platform.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.490
Think of it as a
platform consisting

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.900
of multiple components.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.550
SaaS is one of them.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.170
R is another.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.330
100% validated at this time.

