play a role in your cloud

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.030
deployments on AWS and how--

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:49.490
have you had any issues
integrating those technology

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:50.330
together?

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:54.140
And also how are you kind
of getting your data science

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:57.170
colleagues, your statistician
colleagues, on board

00:13:57.170 --> 00:14:00.710
with using these platforms
that depend on these services?

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.750
If you could elaborate on that.

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:04.070
Certainly.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:06.260
So I mentioned the AWS.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.780
But one of the key
components of this

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:11.750
is containerization, Eric, as
you rightfully pointed out.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.340
Yeah, I was waiting to--

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.070
I was hoping that somebody
would ask that question.

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:19.610
So everything in a
validated environment,

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:24.530
because the need or the ask
is to ensure repeatability

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:26.070
and reproducibility.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:30.140
The only logical choice at
this time is containers.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:33.140
We try to containerize.

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.130
Well, we have to.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:35.290
It's not that we need to.

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.130
We have to containerize.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.820
So the SPACE platform,
I have a responsibility

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.300
for the R part of it.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.020
So I'm going to concentrate
a little bit more on that.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:48.770
So one, users would
want to basically run

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.720
through their studies
using R. And how

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.410
do we do it is the question.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:58.550
So we are using the project
workbench as our launch pad.

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:02.480
This is deployed on a
traditional EC2 instance,

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.780
and then either we
run ad hoc, just

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:10.230
to ensure for the
users this is possible.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:11.580
This is the production platform.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.260
But there is a logical grouping,
where our users would first

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:18.080
want to come and test it
in a development area,

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.800
and then move it into the
traditional QA and prod.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:26.000
But the way they would first
test in the development area

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.290
is, number one, the entire
RStudio Posit Workbench

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:31.520
is containerized.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:35.630
So we always ensure that we
try to keep up with the latest

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:36.620
release of Posit.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.770
I think we are just one
release behind at this time.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.860
And there are some unique
challenges around it.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:46.400
The challenges are mostly
about the IQ, OQ, and the time

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:47.120
that it takes.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:49.790
For example, it's
not just positive.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:53.960
There are other vendors who are
helping us with this effort.

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:58.130
We have to go through a formal
process of vetting, validating,

00:15:58.130 --> 00:15:58.910
and verifying.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:00.570
So all of this takes time.

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:02.240
So typically, we lag behind.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:04.760
That's one challenge
that we have.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:07.370
Number two, because
we have containerized,

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.940
it gives our users
the capability

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.190
to test something
that is locked on

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:17.810
and be ensured that, again, from
a repeatability perspective,

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.670
containers are the only way
that will help them with that.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:24.620
So first, we will go
through defining the process

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:27.560
and specifying the
requirements as to what really

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:29.090
needs to go into the container.

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:34.790
For example, if our users have
identified a set of workflows

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:39.020
that they would want, to help
them with the clinical studies

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:41.870
that they are looking
into, traditionally, it

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:43.760
will map to a set of--

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.710
if I have to wear
a tactical hat,

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:49.730
it is eventually going to
boil down to R packages.

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:53.130
The R packages that need to
make it into this container.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.550
So this is where
we pretty much are

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.070
using standard
technologies and tools that

00:16:58.070 --> 00:16:59.400
are used in the industry.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.520
We have a Jenkins pipeline.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.520
We are using the
passive workbench to--

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:07.430
sorry, the package
manager to lock down

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:09.560
versions of our
packages that we would

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.829
want to go into the container.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.180
And there are some
business processes

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:21.770
that are defined as to what
should go into this container,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.339
in terms of R packages.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.300
Once we have defined, we run
through the build process

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:29.060
to create the container.

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:35.180
And we will verify it in the
traditional RStudio Workbench.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:39.140
Once our users are
comfortable that, yes, this

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:41.240
is the workflow, this
fits of our model

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:42.800
and we don't see any issues.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.540
That container is then blocked.

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:47.810
And then it moves into
the QA slash Prod area.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:52.190
And that's where they
would run through the--

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:55.550
and that is what is
deemed as a validated run.

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:59.570
Anything that the users would
try out in their development

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:02.660
environment is a
prerequisite, but it is not

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:04.370
deemed as a validated run.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.580
It is only when it is run
through the production

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:09.610
that it is deemed as validated.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:13.750
In order to do that, not
all of these validated runs

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:16.480
behind the scenes will run
as a service account user,

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:19.600
because we want to
ensure that, number one,

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:22.270
no particular user runs
it in their environment

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:24.370
and it runs in a
service account.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:29.890
Behind the scenes, we also have
the traditional EKS cluster,

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:34.180
and where we scale up and
launch these containers.

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:40.150
Thank you, Satish.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:41.980
I was just going
to say in the chat,

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:46.420
I am definitely still learning
all of the AWS acronyms too.

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:49.750
So if anybody wants to
share those in the chat too,

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:51.430
that's really helpful.

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:55.918
One part of a question
earlier that we had missed,

00:18:55.918 --> 00:18:58.210
and I think it would be really
helpful to cover as well

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:01.420
is, what role does a
partner like a [? Atoris ?]

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:05.440
play in facilitating
the AWS deployment?

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:06.520
OK.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:10.540
We pretty much rely on
Tau We are partnering

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:13.180
with [? Atoris ?] mainly
for the validation

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:16.210
aspect of it, Rachael.

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:20.260
And what I mean by
that is, all of the R

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:23.710
packages that we
want to qualify,

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.620
[? Atoris ?] is
helping us with writing

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:31.450
the IQ, OQs that need to run.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:34.000
For example, when we
build the container,

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.640
yes, we have a
list of R packages.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:41.660
But how do we ensure that they
fit the user's requirements?

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:45.160
So one advantage--
one unique thing

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.610
that we have done
inside this is,

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:52.990
we do joint collaboration
when we build the container,

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:55.870
and we run through
the automatic process

00:19:55.870 --> 00:20:00.490
of running through the IQ, OQ
steps that has been written

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:02.320
by the [? Atoris ?] team.

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:05.800
The advantage is, once you
go through the container

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:10.150
build process, one, we are--
as I mentioned earlier, we

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.880
are verifying, saying that these
are the list of our packages

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:13.930
that get installed.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.240
But at the same time,
the scripts will

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:20.770
run during the build process,
which runs through these IQ,

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:21.640
OQ steps.

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:25.540
And at the end of the day,
it will generate a validation

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:29.620
report, where we go
through each and every IQ

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:31.300
step that has been written.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:34.300
It could be Install
Package A, then

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:37.060
ensure that all of
the functionality

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.030
within that package
fits our requirements.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:44.590
And this is where the
OQ part of it helps.

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:47.073
So we run through
the build process.

00:20:47.073 --> 00:20:48.490
And then at the
end of the day, we

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.200
inspect the validation report.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:51.340
Then we take a look.

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:54.670
Hey, did all of the
test cases pass?

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:56.830
If it failed, what
was the reason?

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.130
Is this because of an
underlying system dependency?

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:03.580
Or is this because some other
additional package that we

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.760
forgot needs to be installed?

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:10.060
Or is it simply because
there was a bug somewhere?

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:11.650
And we go through this process.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:13.580
It's an iterative process.

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.580
This is where
[? Atoris ?] is basically

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:18.130
building the entire
IQ, OQ part of it

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:23.045
and taking responsibility
for the validation aspect.

00:21:23.045 --> 00:21:23.670
That's great.

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:24.670
Thank you.

