00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:49.040
as data scientists and
statisticians request support

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:52.740
for open source tools, that
are new to their organization,

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:55.380
how do you think about this?

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:59.900
So thinking through tech
data or other issues,

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:04.400
they're trying to understand
your IT's perspective

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.860
from the requests
that you receive.

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:08.090
So yes.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:11.660
So one, there is a
fairly easy part of it.

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:14.120
That is, what do
you need this for?

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:15.140
Why do you need it?

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:19.070
And that's a simpler question,
because there will always

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:21.350
be a convincing
argument from the users.

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:25.310
Where they'll say, I need it to
solve this particular problem.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:29.010
However, the other part of it
is, as I mentioned earlier,

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.190
we do have to go through
a security process.

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:33.980
Where some of
these build process

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:37.340
that I mentioned earlier,
where we go through it,

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.910
behind the scenes, they come
in and scan the container.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:44.000
Where they trigger a
security validation

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:47.250
report to check for
vulnerabilities.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:48.590
And then they will flag it.

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:52.070
If it's a showstopper,
we have to address it.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:52.970
No questions asked.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:54.150
We have to address it.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.230
It could be a simple question
of installing a newer version

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.240
of the system dependency.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:01.670
And if our validation
fails at that reason,

00:24:01.670 --> 00:24:05.000
then we will somehow have to
ensure that all the checks

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:08.360
and balances are-- basically,
we pass all the checks.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:10.980
So when a request for
a new tool comes in,

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:15.440
we also have to check with
our internal security team

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:17.750
to see if there are any--

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.540
if they have any concerns.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.730
Most of the times, you
know, yeah, it's OK.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:25.110
But sometimes, they
will alert us saying,

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:28.880
hey, do you know that there is
a vulnerability risk with it?

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:31.880
Here is the reason why we
are going to stop this.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:36.140
Most often, they will
also suggest a workaround,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:39.510
or to see how we
can address that.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.710
But when a request for
a new tool comes in,

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:44.840
or when a new R
package comes in,

00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:48.710
we have to go through
these security checks, just

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.860
to ensure that we
don't expose ourselves

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:54.080
for any vulnerability.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:55.250
Yes, it is true.

00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:57.500
Things are sitting
behind a firewall.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:58.340
Yes, it is true.

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.980
They are all running
inside a container, where

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:04.880
the chances are a lot
less than when it runs

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:07.850
in a traditional data center.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:12.830
But we do have to ensure that we
comply with all of the security

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:14.510
risks and challenges.

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:18.830
Thank you very much.

