would also want to learn from
others' experience, as well.

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:55.220
Mike mentioned the internally
developed R packages.

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:57.830
So they are also contributing
to the open source,

00:26:57.830 --> 00:26:59.160
as well, at this time.

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:02.540
So there is both
a give and take.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:04.020
Learn from each other.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:06.770
So for the users,
it was pretty easy,

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:09.860
and they were the ones who
were saying, hey, we want this,

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:15.170
because this is going to help
us get to where we want, simply

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:16.940
because of the open
source contribution

00:27:16.940 --> 00:27:22.250
and because things were much
faster when it came to R.

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:28.970
When it came to the TQ team R,
internal quality team, number

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:32.630
one, they wanted
to ensure, one, we

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:36.890
were basically putting a
checkmark against everything

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:38.150
that they asked for.

00:27:38.150 --> 00:27:39.620
This was new for them.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:41.150
We had to sit with them.

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:43.400
For example, the
dockable process

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:46.970
that I mentioned earlier,
they had several questions.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.970
We had to prove it to
them that, no matter

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:52.520
what, what I run today,
if I have to come back

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:55.280
and run it again tomorrow,
except for the timestamp

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:58.340
that it was built, we
had to show and prove

00:27:58.340 --> 00:28:00.620
that it was going
through the same process.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.750
No matter how many
times we rebuild,

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:05.090
we were going to get
the exact same result.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:07.490
Yes, there will be some
challenges along the way.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.580
It wasn't easy, because
we were also new to it.

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:13.220
But we were able to
prove it to them.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:15.170
And it required many--

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.240
I want to say weeks,
months of effort,

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:18.320
trying to convince them.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:21.770
Because again, sometimes
when you are in the vanguard,

00:28:21.770 --> 00:28:26.870
the onus is on you to prove
that what you are doing

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:30.290
is not violating any norms,
well-established norms

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:31.190
and practices.

00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:33.990
So it took us some time.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:38.570
So once they were convinced
that the tools and technologies

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:41.660
that we had used conformed
to their requirements,

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:43.400
they gave their
seal of approval.

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:45.410
Because they were also
learning in this process.

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.900
Because again, with
cloud technology,

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:50.180
sometimes they'll ask
questions like, hey, what

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:52.160
is your fail back mechanism?

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:54.050
What happens if things go down?

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:57.020
And as I mentioned
earlier, we are all on AWS.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:00.500
So we had to tell them,
you have the power of AWS.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:03.470
We wouldn't want to worry about
something going down or having

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:05.960
catastrophic-- but yet,
we had to plan for it.

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:06.470
Right?

00:29:06.470 --> 00:29:09.050
We had to plan for it.

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:12.350
And then even something
as simple as disaster

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:13.220
and recovery, right?

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.140
How do you recover from it?

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:17.960
It's not like you could go and
build something immediately

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:19.340
in another data center.

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:24.170
Because we are locked on
to a particular AWS region,

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:28.910
which is US [INAUDIBLE] One that
is extremely popular for most

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.530
of the folks out here.

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:32.820
[? USC1 ?] Northern
Virginia region.

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:36.080
So we had to basically
convince them

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.660
that, yes, there are
some well-founded fears.

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:43.100
But no, we don't have to be
totally paranoid about it.

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:46.850
The question about the
health regulatory agencies,

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:50.000
and how did we
convince them, I'm

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.190
going to answer this in
a slightly different way.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:55.400
If you talk to any
health agency as such,

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.100
they're not going to say you
have to restrict yourself

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:01.280
to R or SaaS, or this
is bad, or that is good,

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.690
or that is better.

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:07.340
We just have to prove it to
them that what we ran, whenever,

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:09.500
when they come and
ask you for evidence,

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.710
that we will be able to
show the exact same results

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:14.700
or the exact same study.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.880
And let me stop here.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:21.610
Because I don't have any
particular experience

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:22.840
with that part of it.

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:25.920
Got it.

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:26.658
Yeah, thanks.

00:30:26.658 --> 00:30:28.200
No, I really appreciate
hearing that.

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:29.200
I think it's impressive.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:33.460
Both really enjoy hearing
about the technological pieces,

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:35.430
but also about how the
organization got there.

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:38.610
I think it's pretty impressive
that the quality and reg

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:40.590
folks have got on
board and you've

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:42.810
been able to wrap that in
all the appropriate change

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:47.290
control and quality process
stuff that they require.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:49.110
So yeah, it's neat
to hear all this.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:50.160
Thanks.

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.220
Thank you.

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:55.280
Thank you for all the great
questions coming in too.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:57.860
I see [? Parth. ?] You had
asked a question earlier.

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.860
Do you want to jump in?

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:03.250
The question was, is
R capable of running

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:07.270
production environments with
large scale data pipelines?

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:10.390
Do you test that capability
while validating a system?

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:15.730
Large scale, right?

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:20.500
So large is probably going to be
a relative term here, Rachael.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:24.550
Many a time, we are going
to rely on our users

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:26.710
to tell us what that is.

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:31.060
Yes, we did see some
challenges initially.

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:34.150
And we are working
through that at this time.

00:31:34.150 --> 00:31:37.060
Because there might
be an odd scenario

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:41.180
that we want to ensure that the
maximum load can be supported.

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:43.990
We are working with, as
you know, with Posit team

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:46.070
as well to help us with that.

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.160
And I feel very
positive that we should

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:54.068
be able to address some
of those issues as well.

00:31:54.068 --> 00:31:56.110
Because at the end of the
day, these requirements

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:58.210
will have to come
in from our users.

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:00.915
And they will define
that we want to ensure--

00:32:00.915 --> 00:32:02.290
I'm going to throw
out a number--

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:07.990
that 100 users concurrently
at the same time, or 400

00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:10.810
as the case may be here, or 500.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:13.420
We want to be in a
position to support that.

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:16.840
Sometimes, as you
know, folks who

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:20.020
are familiar with
EKS [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:23.800
we start scaling up,
there are some delays.

00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:25.510
It could lead to
some frustration

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:30.490
on the users, where, instead
of seeing things respond

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:34.060
in a second or two, if
it takes even 30 seconds,

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:36.170
30 seconds is way
too much for them.

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.520
What can we do to
help alleviate that?

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.830
There are certain
tools and technologies

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:45.230
that we are still
evaluating at this time.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:47.170
And for those of
you folks who are

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.090
completely familiar
and comfortable

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:53.710
with EKS, Carpenter, to
help us with auto scaling.

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:57.370
That is something that we
are evaluating at this time.

00:32:57.370 --> 00:32:59.320
Again, as I said,
everything has to be

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:01.450
blessed from our
security team, and

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:04.240
from our internal
compliance team as well.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:07.090
Because even if we
have to implement

00:33:07.090 --> 00:33:11.860
what might be regarded as a
small change on the platform,

00:33:11.860 --> 00:33:16.060
because these are what I
consider validated platforms,

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:19.570
there is extensive scrutiny
that we are subjected to.

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:22.030
Mainly in terms
of documentation.

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:25.760
Sometimes, it takes a little
longer than we would want.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:27.970
For example, we
could deploy things

00:33:27.970 --> 00:33:31.060
in our development environment
where it's pretty much open.

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:34.240
We can go and evaluate whatever
we want, implement it there,

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:36.250
and then we will
find out, oh my gosh,

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:38.050
it took us only two
minutes to do this,

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:40.450
but it is going to
take a couple of hours,

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:43.150
simply because we
have to provide proof

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:46.990
at each and every step, so that
if we have to replicate this

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:51.340
at a later time, we have all
of those protocols in place.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.400
So yeah, it will take a little
bit longer to get there.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:57.610
But that is one challenge
that we have today.

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:02.190
Thank you.

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:05.910
One of the Slido questions-- and
I know we touched a bit already

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:08.469
on the collaboration that
happens between teams,

00:34:08.469 --> 00:34:10.889
especially with security--

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:13.679
but do you have any tips
about working internally

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:16.800
with your peers and
leadership to invest in moving

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:18.239
towards new platforms?

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:23.320
Yes.

00:34:23.320 --> 00:34:27.639
One of the things that we
have to be cognizant of

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:31.090
is the cost aspect of it, right?

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:34.600
When we do things in
the cloud in [? One, ?]

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:38.980
we absolutely need to have
our leadership's support.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:43.480
That is because with
in-house data centers,

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:46.150
with in-house equipment,
there is a finite cost

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:48.100
that is associated
with it, whether it

00:34:48.100 --> 00:34:51.739
is the initial capital cost
or the maintenance cost of it.

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:54.969
We know what it is, and we
can always project that.

00:34:54.969 --> 00:34:58.450
The challenge with AWS, or
with any of the cloud service

00:34:58.450 --> 00:34:59.380
providers--

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:02.740
I want to say cloud provider--
is you cannot predict those

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:03.730
charges upfront.

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:08.570
Because you generally tend
to pay after the fact.

00:35:08.570 --> 00:35:11.792
So I could-- for
example, the load testing

00:35:11.792 --> 00:35:13.000
that we talked about earlier.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:17.290
How much can we go and how
much loads can we test?

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:20.140
If we have to start testing,
then we will automatically

00:35:20.140 --> 00:35:21.460
start scaling up.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:22.880
We won't realize it.

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:26.410
And then at the end of the
month when we are handed a bill,

00:35:26.410 --> 00:35:27.460
and we're like, what?

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:29.500
Did I incur so much of cost?

00:35:29.500 --> 00:35:31.600
They don't tell us
here the reason why.

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:33.250
That is a unique challenge.

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:38.110
But on the other side of it,
consider a post-COVID world

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:41.110
where we were completely
shielded because none of us

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:43.240
ever came to a
[? Janssen ?] location.

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:44.830
We were prepping this remote.

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:46.450
We were 100% functional.

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:49.990
And all of that was because
we were in the cloud.

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:52.850
So there are certain
benefits, as well.

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:56.110
The cost part of it, you have
to have management support.

00:35:56.110 --> 00:35:58.960
The huge advantage
with that is now,

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:04.120
hey, something happened with the
instance in your data center.

00:36:04.120 --> 00:36:06.370
Somebody has to go
and physically verify

00:36:06.370 --> 00:36:09.070
whether it is pulling out
a hard disk, replacing it.

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:13.510
All of that would cause
substantial delays.

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:16.773
With any of the cloud providers,
you don't have that challenge.

00:36:16.773 --> 00:36:18.190
Something happened,
you stop that.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:19.240
You start it back up.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:21.280
Because that gives
you all the tools

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:23.920
to be back up and
running in no time.

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:29.020
So I see there's a
conversation happening

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:31.600
in the chat here, and
I can't really read it.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:34.000
But I know it started
off Mike's question.

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:39.190
So Mike, I'd love to
have you asked that too.

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:40.000
Thanks.

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:42.390
Satish, thanks.

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.140
It's nice to hear about
another big pharma

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:48.100
company using containers and
the qualification process.

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:51.370
I've been at the sharp end
of that for many years.

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:54.670
And from my perspective,
it's fantastic to be

00:36:54.670 --> 00:36:58.180
able to qualify at once and
then deploy it and use it

00:36:58.180 --> 00:36:59.990
in many, many places.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:01.970
It's one of the big
strengths of this approach.

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:04.000
But how do you
balance up the kind

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:09.730
of slow and steady approach,
of qualification, deployment,

00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:11.590
keeping things
nice and, you know,

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:16.570
documented, and nice and
steady, versus colleagues

00:37:16.570 --> 00:37:19.720
who want latest greatest,
give me it tomorrow, I've

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:22.360
got an urgent deadline?

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:29.810
Yeah, so let me talk about
from a validated platform

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:30.310
perspective.

00:37:30.310 --> 00:37:32.360
Because I think it's relevant.

00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:35.260
Yes, we do get
asked that question.

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:38.710
For example, for one of
the more recent studies,

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:41.380
it did require us to
install a newer version--

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:46.390
new package, which required
some system dependencies.

00:37:46.390 --> 00:37:51.220
And there is no
instantaneous answer.

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:54.040
That's sadly-- you know, when
you have to containerize,

00:37:54.040 --> 00:37:57.550
you have to balance your
long term prospects,

00:37:57.550 --> 00:38:00.730
saying that, yes, there
is a short term pain,

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:03.070
but there is a long term gain.

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:05.830
And yes, it does frustrate
our users sometimes,

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:09.790
because we will not be
able to react to it simply

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:12.460
because, again, when you go
through this build process,

00:38:12.460 --> 00:38:14.380
you will encounter challenges.

00:38:14.380 --> 00:38:15.850
Trust me.

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:20.260
But where I see a
silver lining is,

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:23.140
we have incredible
partners, both from Posit

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:24.850
as well as [? Atoris, ?]
who are helping

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:26.350
Janssen with that approach.

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:26.860
Right?

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:30.010
So any time we have a
challenge-- for example,

00:38:30.010 --> 00:38:33.580
when we deployed
our Workbench, we--

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:36.100
due to the nature of--
every organization

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:39.340
will have their own unique
challenges, I promise you that.

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:40.760
Every organization.

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:43.240
One of the things that we
had internally debugged

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:45.880
was how we were treating
our clinical data in terms

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:48.190
of active directory groups.

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:49.660
A little bit more tactical.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:54.370
But when we realized that the
initial deployment of Workbench

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:57.550
did not work out, they were
able to quickly come up

00:38:57.550 --> 00:39:00.910
with a release and then come
up with an update saying,

00:39:00.910 --> 00:39:04.430
here is how we are going
to help you get that.

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:06.490
So we were able to
deploy a new version.

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:09.073
There it wasn't a
question of "if,"

00:39:09.073 --> 00:39:10.240
it was a question of "when."

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:14.770
We wanted, and we were able
to, adopt that quickly--

00:39:14.770 --> 00:39:15.850
fairly quickly.

00:39:15.850 --> 00:39:19.090
But then the process that
it takes to get there,

00:39:19.090 --> 00:39:21.580
documenting each
and every thing,

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.410
that there is no shortcut.

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:25.960
I'm afraid there's
absolutely no shortcut.

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:30.670
Because our TQ team wants proof
that, hey, show us evidence

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:31.900
that what you did.

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:37.010
So there's extensive IQ, OQ
that we go through, as well.

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:37.810
Yeah.

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:39.790
There is absolutely no shortcut.

00:39:39.790 --> 00:39:44.380
But we always have to tell what
is right, do the right thing,

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:45.820
as everybody says.

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:48.040
Saying that, yes, there
is a short term pain,

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:51.360
but there is a long
term gain there.

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:52.860
I would also just
add that there's

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:56.500
a completely separate business
unit consideration for this,

00:39:56.500 --> 00:39:57.000
as well.

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:58.740
Because Mike, with
you at Pfizer,

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:00.540
you're also another
group that has

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:02.580
a large group of SaaS users.

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:05.940
And the target group that this
is going out to in Janssen

00:40:05.940 --> 00:40:07.530
is that SaaS business unit.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:11.220
So, Satish is just one part
of this R adoption effort

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:13.650
that goes into the
packages that are--

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:15.630
[INAUDIBLE] was just
released open source.

00:40:15.630 --> 00:40:17.422
But that was designed
to be able to support

00:40:17.422 --> 00:40:19.917
Janssen standards to be
able to live up to all

00:40:19.917 --> 00:40:21.000
of that within that group.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.970
But then you have all
of these business users

00:40:23.970 --> 00:40:25.830
who have gone
through SaaS training

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:27.700
to get on board with that.

00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:29.700
And if you start changing
everything underfoot

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:33.130
without bringing them
along with you too quickly,

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:37.020
then that will also
upset the business users

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:38.380
in a different way.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:40.140
So there's definitely
a balance to strike

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:44.460
that you need to consider from
an organizational standpoint.

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.710
Yeah, see-- sorry, go ahead.

00:40:46.710 --> 00:40:50.910
I think the kind of folks
involved in reporting clinical

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:55.800
trials data want it to be
nice, controlled, well-managed,

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.100
slow changing, predictable.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:05.700
It's other groups who have
more rapid turnaround--

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:08.760
the statisticians,
in some senses,

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:10.290
supporting those
groups, that want

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:12.563
the kind of latest greatest.

00:41:12.563 --> 00:41:13.980
This just emerged
on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:17.140
Now I want it because
it solves my problem.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:19.870
But I think, like
Satish is saying,

00:41:19.870 --> 00:41:23.410
that the process
to just simply fold

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:25.450
that into the
existing framework,

00:41:25.450 --> 00:41:28.420
it's not as simple
as many people think.

00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:30.430
So I'm agreeing.

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:31.300
Yeah.

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:32.410
It never is.

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:35.450
It will take some
time to get us there.

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:41.090
But the one thing that the users
absolutely want is stability.

00:41:41.090 --> 00:41:45.760
We don't want to introduce
changes which will potentially

00:41:45.760 --> 00:41:47.690
break things.

00:41:47.690 --> 00:41:48.970
So that's a red flag.

00:41:48.970 --> 00:41:49.750
That's a no no.

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.350
And this is where we spend
a careful amount of time,

00:41:53.350 --> 00:41:55.570
a substantial amount
of time, ensuring

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:59.320
that care is given to ensuring
backward compatibility.

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:00.880
We don't break things.

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:02.710
And this all, at
the end of the day,

00:42:02.710 --> 00:42:08.350
will depend on how much of IQ,
OQ are willing to go through.

00:42:08.350 --> 00:42:12.860
How much of effort we
are going to put into it.

00:42:12.860 --> 00:42:16.220
And I think it is well worth it.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:19.400
I'm getting the feeling that
this is a topic we definitely

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:20.720
want to stay on.

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:23.970
There's a lot of
thoughts on here too.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:27.850
I see, Eric, I think you added
in-- you said you had something

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:28.850
to comment on here, too.

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:31.446
You want to jump in?

00:42:31.446 --> 00:42:33.470
Yeah, I had a few of them.

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:37.330
But I think the key points
that I was commenting on

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:40.390
are that containerization,
we're looking

00:42:40.390 --> 00:42:43.300
at this from kind of
the broader level,

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:46.810
for an organization's
analytical platform.

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:49.090
I'm interested,
Satish, if you've

00:42:49.090 --> 00:42:52.870
been keeping an eye on things
like GitHub code spaces,

00:42:52.870 --> 00:42:55.930
where it's giving, at
a repository level,

00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:59.277
the ability to containerize
its environment.

00:42:59.277 --> 00:43:01.360
I mean, you can do that
without GitHub code space,

00:43:01.360 --> 00:43:02.500
as well, I suppose.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:04.810
But I'm just curious
where you find--

00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:11.020
where your opinion is on
maybe those other analysts

00:43:11.020 --> 00:43:13.510
or statisticians that
want to be on the cutting

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:15.250
edge for a particular
project, and they

00:43:15.250 --> 00:43:17.950
try to take matters in their
own hands, if they have a little

00:43:17.950 --> 00:43:21.250
know how of how containers
work with Docker and the like,

00:43:21.250 --> 00:43:23.260
compared to what
you're trying to do

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:25.960
from this more broader
effort, which is obviously

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:28.320
hugely valuable too.

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:29.670
Yeah.

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:32.430
One thing that I must
have forgotten to mention

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:36.910
is, even with the containers
that internally get deployed,

00:43:36.910 --> 00:43:41.340
our technology services
team will tell us

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:44.580
what we could use
as a base, right?

00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:47.760
In other words, we just cannot
go and pull anything from

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:48.330
Docker hub.

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:50.250
It has to be validated again.

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:52.330
Because they want
to do a scan of it.

00:43:52.330 --> 00:43:57.930
So there are some, I want to
say, well-defined requirements.

00:43:57.930 --> 00:43:59.430
I don't want to
call it constraints.

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:01.950
It could be as
simple as, you have

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:04.770
to use this version of
[INAUDIBLE] containers.

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:06.970
I'm just throwing
out as an example.

00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:09.330
You can't simply
go and use Ubuntu

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:13.800
unless it is supported
inside our J&amp;amp;J ecosystem.

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:16.950
That is, I'm sure folks are
familiar with Artifactory.

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:21.060
Artifactory is where we
host our container images

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:25.150
and where we also pull
certain base images as well.

00:44:25.150 --> 00:44:27.570
So to answer your
question, Eric,

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:30.840
we cannot go and pull
directly from GitHub.

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:33.180
But to answer a
second part of it--

00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:36.960
say, for example, if you were
to point me to some GitHub repo,

00:44:36.960 --> 00:44:38.100
can you support this?

00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:39.930
The short answer is maybe.

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:42.480
And The reason I said
maybe is we can go and take

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:45.720
a look into it, see what
software modules are

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:48.180
being installed as a
part of it towards--

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:49.320
in the container.

00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:53.040
See what it is that we
could immediately use.

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:56.910
Or if it requires some sort
of a substitution by-- say,

00:44:56.910 --> 00:44:59.340
for example, some other
equivalent package--

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:01.560
then we will go and
download it, install it.

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:05.190
All of that, so long as it
is blessed by our technology

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:07.390
services team, we'll
be able to do it.

00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:12.130
So the same challenge exists
for our users as well.

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:15.570
They have to rely on somebody
in IT to help build them that.

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:17.640
Yes, we are deploying pipelines.

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:21.450
For example, Mike referred
to the internally developed

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:23.610
package as well.

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:26.520
We are now
establishing pipelines

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:30.540
where users, from the business
partners that we work with,

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:33.070
can develop a mod package.

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:37.110
And by simply doing the cut and
paste of the basic necessities

00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:38.400
of the pipeline.

00:45:38.400 --> 00:45:40.020
We use Jenkins pipeline.

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:41.970
We call it Jenkins
Pipeline Manager.

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:45.030
So they'll take that part
of the infrastructure code,

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:47.910
replicate it, and
then work on what

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:49.590
they are best qualified to do.

00:45:49.590 --> 00:45:51.840
That is, work on the
art package and not

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:54.420
have to worry about the
mundane aspects of, oh,

00:45:54.420 --> 00:45:55.290
how do I build it?

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:56.400
How do mint it?

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:58.620
Because all of that is
streamlined to a point

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:01.440
where we run through the
limiting, unit testing process.

00:46:01.440 --> 00:46:03.960
All of [INAUDIBLE] in an
art package is being built.

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:07.900
So we help them by deploying
those pipelines as well.

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:12.370
So we make it sort
of easier on them,

00:46:12.370 --> 00:46:16.230
but sometimes I might
be trivializing things.

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:19.080
Some users might find
it a little bit more

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:22.020
challenging for their test.

00:46:22.020 --> 00:46:26.580
But we are trying to templatize
it to minimize disruption

00:46:26.580 --> 00:46:28.740
to the users.

00:46:28.740 --> 00:46:32.628
To minimize learnings
for our users.

00:46:32.628 --> 00:46:34.170
Because that's
infrastructure, right?

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:36.730
I mean, they might
be focused more

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:39.070
on clinical trial
reporting efforts

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:41.500
than have to worry about
mundane activities like, oh,

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:42.580
how do I build this?

00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:43.450
Because that's IT.

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:47.240
Thank you.

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:48.157
That's very helpful.

00:46:48.157 --> 00:46:50.630
And I consider myself one
of maybe four or five people

00:46:50.630 --> 00:46:52.880
in my entire
organization that likes

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:54.480
both sides of that spectrum.

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:56.480
But we can't expect
all the statistician

00:46:56.480 --> 00:46:59.140
to be as geeky about
this as people like me.

00:46:59.140 --> 00:47:00.680
Yeah.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:06.050
Nick, I see you had a great
comment on building adoption.

00:47:06.050 --> 00:47:09.380
And I'd love to spend a few
minutes talking about that too

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:12.570
if you want to jump in.

00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:13.070
Sure.

00:47:13.070 --> 00:47:16.040
Is there a specific question?

00:47:16.040 --> 00:47:18.020
Well, no, I just
saw you were talking

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:23.750
about educating the users
about validation, as well.

00:47:23.750 --> 00:47:24.350
Right, yeah.

00:47:24.350 --> 00:47:27.200
Yeah, this goes back to
Mike's question around,

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:30.380
how do you deal with validated
versus having the latest

00:47:30.380 --> 00:47:30.930
greatest?

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:32.388
Yeah, I just threw
in my two cents.

00:47:32.388 --> 00:47:35.510
I think the big part from
our side is really education.

00:47:35.510 --> 00:47:38.930
So I guess [INAUDIBLE]
kind of mentioned,

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:40.850
as well, a lot of the
users don't really care.

00:47:40.850 --> 00:47:44.160
But you have to help them
understand this is difficult,

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:44.660
right?

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:47.450
It's not just, I want
this, let's have it.

00:47:47.450 --> 00:47:51.500
We have to consider accuracy,
reproducibility, traceability

00:47:51.500 --> 00:47:52.680
of these things.

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:54.680
Most important is that
our results are correct.

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:56.450
And they understand that, right?

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:59.110
So that's kind of what we
used to make sure like,

00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:01.100
OK, we have to validate this.

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:02.510
Here's what it takes to do it.

00:48:02.510 --> 00:48:04.790
It's significant effort.

00:48:04.790 --> 00:48:06.090
So that takes a little time.

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:07.970
But then really use case driven.

00:48:07.970 --> 00:48:09.663
So sometimes people
ask for things

00:48:09.663 --> 00:48:10.830
just because they want them.

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:12.920
But then when you said, well,
what do you want to use it for?

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:15.503
You start getting more details
that they don't really need it.

00:48:15.503 --> 00:48:18.830
So having that use case driven
validation-- what do you

00:48:18.830 --> 00:48:19.430
need it for?

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:20.565
Why do you need it?

00:48:20.565 --> 00:48:22.190
Is it for production
or is it something

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:25.040
you could just use as like an
exploratory environment, right?

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:27.230
Do we really have
to validate this?

00:48:27.230 --> 00:48:29.510
And that really shortens
the list a lot more.

00:48:29.510 --> 00:48:33.440
And then once we get to,
OK, we get it, you need it,

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:35.960
we don't have the expertise
as like the team that's

00:48:35.960 --> 00:48:37.190
overseeing it.

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:40.397
You're now sort of our
de facto expert on this.

00:48:40.397 --> 00:48:41.480
Put some skin in the game.

00:48:41.480 --> 00:48:43.583
Help us out with this.

00:48:43.583 --> 00:48:45.500
And then, sometimes,
they're willing to do it.

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:47.480
Versus maybe they
say, well I see

00:48:47.480 --> 00:48:49.610
you've already validated
the-- the stats

00:48:49.610 --> 00:48:50.990
package is approved for use.

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:52.623
I could do the stats
package instead.

00:48:52.623 --> 00:48:53.915
Let me just use that, you know?

00:48:53.915 --> 00:48:58.390
And it sort of helps again
shorten that list out.

00:48:58.390 --> 00:48:59.020
Thank you.

00:48:59.020 --> 00:49:00.070
That's really helpful.

00:49:00.070 --> 00:49:02.140
Sure thing.

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:05.500
Travis, I realize I had missed
a question of yours earlier,

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:10.030
and I wanted to have you jump
in next with your question.

00:49:10.030 --> 00:49:11.910
Did I ask a question
that wasn't answered?

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:14.400
I think you did.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:16.830
So it was earlier
when I missed it.

00:49:16.830 --> 00:49:21.330
But it was about, are
there any open source IQ

00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:23.490
[? OQ ?] pipelines for R?

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:24.450
Oh yeah, yeah.

00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:25.980
Yeah, Mike had some
good commentary

00:49:25.980 --> 00:49:27.995
in the chat around that too.

00:49:27.995 --> 00:49:29.370
Yeah, because I
mean I guess sort

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:31.890
of the source of
my question was,

00:49:31.890 --> 00:49:36.210
for many use cases in clinical
research like we're doing,

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:41.340
it would seem that the IQ, OQ
need not be overly complicated.

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:44.130
Because if we just want to
ensure that R was properly

00:49:44.130 --> 00:49:48.780
installed and can run,
[INAUDIBLE] pipelines, can run

00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:51.450
a regression, a time-to-event
analysis without trouble.

00:49:51.450 --> 00:49:53.370
We're not doing anything fancy.

00:49:53.370 --> 00:49:57.630
It would seem, couldn't I just
do some minimal set of checks?

00:49:57.630 --> 00:50:01.690
For which someone might write
a reasonable starting point?

00:50:01.690 --> 00:50:04.050
In the open source
realm for like,

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:05.910
has R actually
properly installed?

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:08.520
And does it actually-- do
these three, four packages

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:11.310
do the thing that
they're supposed to do?

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:14.100
Just curious about what a
minimum viable product for that

00:50:14.100 --> 00:50:14.860
might look like.

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.920
But this is, of course,
a [? Atoris' ?] realm

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:17.640
of expertise.

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:21.690
So I really don't know what
those answers would look like.

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:25.110
Yeah, and just a note, one
of the things that we've really

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:27.720
taken the guidance
from is the work

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:29.310
that was done in
the R validation hub

00:50:29.310 --> 00:50:32.910
to start defining what a risk
based approach to validation

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:34.650
is around that.

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:37.650
And for things like
a lot of the base R

00:50:37.650 --> 00:50:42.870
stuff, a lot of the tidyverse
maintained packages,

00:50:42.870 --> 00:50:44.370
those are--

00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:46.440
Nick, what is the term
that we landed on--

00:50:46.440 --> 00:50:50.610
accepted for that, where you
can run the package unit tests

00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:52.315
and you can accept that?

00:50:52.315 --> 00:50:54.690
But again, part of this is
also documenting the evidence.

00:50:54.690 --> 00:50:56.580
But that was done so
that you can pass those

00:50:56.580 --> 00:51:00.840
through Satish's team, get the
report so that we can log that

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.720
into the QA systems, so
that you have the evidence

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:06.210
and traceability
that that was done.

00:51:06.210 --> 00:51:08.970
But then, like Nick says, when
you get into other things,

00:51:08.970 --> 00:51:11.220
and especially getting into
the more statistical realm

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:13.740
of things, having use
case driven approaches

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:16.020
around what you're
looking for, how you can

00:51:16.020 --> 00:51:18.370
defend the integrity of that.

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:20.883
And then, my
comment back to you,

00:51:20.883 --> 00:51:22.050
you're saying that it works.

00:51:22.050 --> 00:51:23.760
What does work mean?

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:26.880
What is the kind of baseline
that you're assessing that

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:27.570
against?

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:29.880
So there's a lot of
questions that you

00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:33.420
need to go in and investigate
and answer that so that you're

00:51:33.420 --> 00:51:34.530
defending yourself.

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:36.570
Because all of
this comes back to,

00:51:36.570 --> 00:51:38.850
what happens if you have
to undergo an audit?

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:41.223
What if a regulatory
agency comes back to you

00:51:41.223 --> 00:51:43.140
and then starts asking
questions of how you're

00:51:43.140 --> 00:51:46.450
verifying what you're doing?

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:49.890
So that's the worst
case scenario,

00:51:49.890 --> 00:51:53.760
and something like this
would be that there--

00:51:53.760 --> 00:51:58.530
something is turned around
because the integrity

00:51:58.530 --> 00:51:59.790
of your system was questioned.

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:04.140
And pharma, I think that
a lot of times pharma

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:07.272
is extremely risk averse, just
because of the investment that

00:52:07.272 --> 00:52:08.730
goes into getting
where things are.

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:11.550
Statistical programming has
an extremely small window

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:14.250
in the larger scope of
everything that's going on.

00:52:14.250 --> 00:52:17.670
And no one wants to be
the person who kind of is

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:19.230
the showstopper for something.

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:22.650
Or even delays can just
be extremely costly.

00:52:22.650 --> 00:52:30.305
But yeah, we're working in an
extremely risk averse industry.

00:52:30.305 --> 00:52:32.430
That's a fascinating
answer, especially regarding

00:52:32.430 --> 00:52:33.810
the unit test for that.

00:52:33.810 --> 00:52:35.220
I mean, it hadn't
occurred to me.

00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:38.760
But it's probably a good
message to developers.

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:40.500
Write good unit tests,
and then there's

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:43.530
actually almost
unintended but fortunate

00:52:43.530 --> 00:52:46.855
downstream consequences of
writing good unit tests.

00:52:46.855 --> 00:52:48.480
So you could just
write out that report

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:50.070
that, for example,
the Tidyverse Unit

00:52:50.070 --> 00:52:53.490
tests, which I know are
comprehensive and good,

00:52:53.490 --> 00:52:54.310
check out.

00:52:54.310 --> 00:52:55.930
That's awesome.

00:52:55.930 --> 00:52:56.430
Yeah.

00:52:56.430 --> 00:52:59.310
And one of the things that at
least our technology services

00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:05.190
team is pushing for is to
ensure that every R package goes

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:06.870
through some static analysis.

00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:11.000
Saying, OK, here's
what's being deployed.

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.820
Did it go through all
the checks and balances?

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:17.450
Sometimes it's impossible and
sometimes it's not possible.

00:53:17.450 --> 00:53:19.220
And it depends on who
you are talking to.

00:53:19.220 --> 00:53:22.970
For the users, does it work
for my particular use case?

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:24.500
Go deploy the package.

00:53:24.500 --> 00:53:26.540
Somebody else comes
in later and finds out

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:28.370
that it's causing a conflict.

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:31.070
That's a red flag.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:34.430
Sometimes, security might
only be looking at it

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:36.170
from a slightly
different perspective.

00:53:36.170 --> 00:53:38.000
They don't care about
the functionality.

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:41.160
They care about the
security aspect of it.

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:43.880
So if it passes all the
vulnerabilities checks,

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:46.430
then you are good to go
from their perspective.

00:53:46.430 --> 00:53:51.500
But go back to this comment that
you had earlier, Travis, that,

00:53:51.500 --> 00:53:52.820
hey, I just installed it.

00:53:52.820 --> 00:53:53.670
Did it work?

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:55.550
I'm going to go and use it.

00:53:55.550 --> 00:53:57.830
There is a danger,
as Mike pointed out.

00:53:57.830 --> 00:54:01.200
I've personally seen
one effort, at least--

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:02.450
I don't want to say it failed.

00:54:02.450 --> 00:54:04.160
But it has reached
a point where nobody

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:07.010
wants to use this because IT
took this approach that, yeah,

00:54:07.010 --> 00:54:09.860
we're going to build this,
gonna do minimal qualification.

00:54:09.860 --> 00:54:11.900
For us, if it installed R, good.

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:13.460
It passed our check.

00:54:13.460 --> 00:54:16.880
Then they said, it's on the
users, like Nick and others,

00:54:16.880 --> 00:54:20.090
to go install the R
packages for what they want.

00:54:20.090 --> 00:54:23.180
And then they suddenly
realize that, as everybody

00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:26.240
knows in the R ecosystem,
most of the packages

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:27.650
are users can install.

00:54:27.650 --> 00:54:30.180
There is this small subset
that is-- sometimes,

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:32.810
it requires a unique
dependency, which is easy.

00:54:32.810 --> 00:54:36.230
But sometimes it does
require things to be handled.

00:54:36.230 --> 00:54:39.380
And that's where the users say,
I don't have this expertise,

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:41.960
and I'm sorry, I
just cannot use this.

00:54:41.960 --> 00:54:46.670
So there is a danger to it when
you do minimum qualification.

00:54:46.670 --> 00:54:48.800
I for one certainly
don't believe in it

00:54:48.800 --> 00:54:50.630
and wouldn't encourage that.

00:54:50.630 --> 00:54:52.970
We have to take our
users' workflow,

00:54:52.970 --> 00:54:56.630
ensure that everything
is tested accurately.

00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:59.180
As Mike pointed out,
it's just to make sure

00:54:59.180 --> 00:55:00.680
that we defend ourselves.

00:55:00.680 --> 00:55:02.690
God forbid if there
is an audit and we

00:55:02.690 --> 00:55:06.650
have to show evidence of what
we ran a couple of years ago.

00:55:06.650 --> 00:55:11.990
Trust me, that short term pain
leading to long term gain,

00:55:11.990 --> 00:55:15.020
it's accurate in this place.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:18.750
Thank you, Satish.

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:21.900
I know we have a few minutes
until the top of the hour here.

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:25.500
Walter, I see you had asked
a question a few minutes ago.

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.000
Do you want to jump
in and ask that?

00:55:32.877 --> 00:55:33.710
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:33.710 --> 00:55:35.260
Yeah, I can hear you.

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:35.800
Right.

00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:39.430
No, I was just curious
because I understand

00:55:39.430 --> 00:55:44.290
that the versioning and the
environment and everything

00:55:44.290 --> 00:55:48.730
for, let's say,
critical packages,

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:51.580
or critical tests that
are maybe developed

00:55:51.580 --> 00:55:55.280
and deployment packages,
need to be in place.

00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:57.530
But I'm working in
the finance industry.

00:55:57.530 --> 00:56:00.550
And so we also have
architecture in place for,

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:04.910
let's say, less critical
like convenience packages.

00:56:04.910 --> 00:56:09.910
And I just wanted to ask
if they are mixing these R,

00:56:09.910 --> 00:56:12.370
using artifact with
maybe the package

00:56:12.370 --> 00:56:19.000
mirror, internal package mirror,
for less critical packages,

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:21.370
like user convenience stuff.

00:56:21.370 --> 00:56:24.100
And these things already take--

00:56:24.100 --> 00:56:27.490
keep everything on the
Posit package manager.

00:56:27.490 --> 00:56:30.340
Just out of curiosity,
because you mentioned

00:56:30.340 --> 00:56:34.000
having Artifactory, having
Jenkins [INAUDIBLE] and things

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:34.630
like that.

00:56:34.630 --> 00:56:37.050
Just [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:37.810
Yeah.

00:56:37.810 --> 00:56:42.120
So we will not distinguish
whether a particular package is

00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:45.180
less important or one is
more important, Walter.

00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:46.560
We just don't take
that approach.

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:50.010
If a user wants a
package, we just

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:53.490
go through the entire
process of IQing, OQing.

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:55.890
Yes, it will be in
Posit package manager.

00:56:55.890 --> 00:56:57.300
Yes, we will snapshot it.

00:56:57.300 --> 00:57:00.630
We will use the exact same
snapshot to replicate it.

00:57:00.630 --> 00:57:05.410
But no, we do not say, hey,
these are less important.

00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:06.480
This is going to--

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:09.390
not go through any qualification
or any such thing like that.

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:13.670
We just go through
the totality of it.

00:57:13.670 --> 00:57:15.760
And as Nick pointed out
earlier, the question

00:57:15.760 --> 00:57:17.980
will always be, why do
you need this package?

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:20.620
And once that question
is answered, from an IT

00:57:20.620 --> 00:57:24.400
perspective, it really doesn't
matter towards the importance

00:57:24.400 --> 00:57:27.130
of that package.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:31.480
Yeah, but I was just
curious, because as I said,

00:57:31.480 --> 00:57:35.740
sometimes it makes sense to
put the full force behind it.

00:57:35.740 --> 00:57:38.860
But sometimes if it's
just like some reporting

00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:41.007
or some small shiny
dashboard that you

00:57:41.007 --> 00:57:42.715
want to share with
colleagues internally,

00:57:42.715 --> 00:57:47.230
it probably may be
overkill to have it

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:48.760
go through the whole process.

00:57:48.760 --> 00:57:51.640
I was curious if you had
this approach, as well.

00:57:51.640 --> 00:57:54.110
Because where I come
from, we have it.

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:55.528
That's why [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:59.020 --> 00:58:00.820
It will be a little
bit hard to treat it

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:03.880
as a validated environment if we
haven't gone through the checks

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:04.867
and balance round.

00:58:08.000 --> 00:58:11.720
And one more question
on packages, too.

00:58:11.720 --> 00:58:18.160
I see Javier you had a question
going back to the containers?

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:19.000
Thanks, Rachael.

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:20.380
Yeah, and I'm sorry.

00:58:20.380 --> 00:58:24.380
I've been in and out with
side conversations with work.

00:58:24.380 --> 00:58:26.800
So my apologies if
this was covered.

00:58:26.800 --> 00:58:30.970
But we have a
similar setup, where

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:35.080
we build containers and
have kind of snapshots

00:58:35.080 --> 00:58:38.630
of RStudio Package Manager.

00:58:38.630 --> 00:58:43.880
And as I guess one of
the heavier users of R,

00:58:43.880 --> 00:58:49.610
at the company, something
that is not a big deal, just

00:58:49.610 --> 00:58:54.320
a weekly annoyance, is that the
versions of the packages that

00:58:54.320 --> 00:58:59.000
are loaded in these containers
are specific versions that

00:58:59.000 --> 00:59:02.190
might have been snapped,
let's say, six months ago.

00:59:02.190 --> 00:59:07.130
So the first thing I do
when I load my RStudio IDE,

00:59:07.130 --> 00:59:12.470
which is in the cloud, is
to update the packages that

00:59:12.470 --> 00:59:13.610
are persisting.

00:59:13.610 --> 00:59:16.910
And then I install
additional packages

00:59:16.910 --> 00:59:23.960
that I need for my workflow,
or [INAUDIBLE] app, et cetera.

00:59:23.960 --> 00:59:29.130
So I was just
curious if you had--

00:59:29.130 --> 00:59:36.090
it'd be excellent if the
packaged versions that

00:59:36.090 --> 00:59:39.690
were kind of booted when
I loaded my RStudio would

00:59:39.690 --> 00:59:42.480
be the current versions
of those packages

00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:45.810
deemed for persistence.

00:59:45.810 --> 00:59:46.935
And that's just something--

00:59:46.935 --> 00:59:49.185
I don't know that there's a
better solution to what we

00:59:49.185 --> 00:59:49.890
have right now.

00:59:49.890 --> 00:59:55.160
I didn't know if there was
maybe a recommendation you had?

00:59:55.160 --> 00:59:57.950
For a validated
environment, the short answer

00:59:57.950 --> 01:00:00.970
is you just have to
rebuild the container.

01:00:00.970 --> 01:00:01.700
Yeah.

01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:06.420
And there's no
other alternative.

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:13.160
However, the process is, if you
need new versions of existing

01:00:13.160 --> 01:00:16.850
packages, then you just
go through the basic IQ

01:00:16.850 --> 01:00:19.530
OQ that was already
written for that package.

01:00:19.530 --> 01:00:21.980
But if there's new functionality
that you want to adopt,

01:00:21.980 --> 01:00:24.860
yes, you will have
to write new IQ OQs.

01:00:24.860 --> 01:00:26.060
No questions asked.

01:00:26.060 --> 01:00:28.730
But there is no shortcut here.

01:00:28.730 --> 01:00:30.500
You just have to
repeat and rinse

01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:32.610
through this entire process.

01:00:32.610 --> 01:00:33.110
Got it.

01:00:33.110 --> 01:00:33.530
OK.

01:00:33.530 --> 01:00:34.030
Thank you.

01:00:34.030 --> 01:00:34.980
I appreciate it.

01:00:34.980 --> 01:00:35.480
Sure.

01:00:35.480 --> 01:00:38.180
And we go through
this whenever we

01:00:38.180 --> 01:00:41.120
have to build a new container
from an IT perspective, right?

01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:43.220
We do check with
users like Nick first.

01:00:43.220 --> 01:00:44.570
Hey, did it meet this?

01:00:44.570 --> 01:00:45.590
Your requirement?

01:00:45.590 --> 01:00:48.620
And they have their own
acceptance testing, as well.

01:00:48.620 --> 01:00:51.170
Then he'll come back and
say, nope, something failed.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:52.340
I need you to look into it.

01:00:52.340 --> 01:00:55.920
Yes, it's in-- basically,
it's repeat and rinse.

01:00:55.920 --> 01:00:56.420
Right?

01:00:56.420 --> 01:00:59.930
So you go through this
process all over again.

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:02.540
So go back to the drawing
board, add the packaging,

01:01:02.540 --> 01:01:05.270
add any system dependencies,
run through your IQ.

01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:06.980
You've released
the container for--

01:01:06.980 --> 01:01:09.260
in development
for users to test.

01:01:09.260 --> 01:01:12.200
And once they say, yep, this all
looks good, it's at that time

01:01:12.200 --> 01:01:14.780
we quote unquote
"lock" the container.

01:01:14.780 --> 01:01:18.380
I know we just passed
the hour mark here.

01:01:18.380 --> 01:01:21.785
And a few people had to
jump off for other meetings.

01:01:21.785 --> 01:01:23.660
I just want to say thank
you so much, Satish,

01:01:23.660 --> 01:01:26.360
for sharing your
experience with us.

01:01:26.360 --> 01:01:29.750
And also the [? Atoris ?]
team for joining.

01:01:29.750 --> 01:01:32.720
Satish, you are such
a wealth of knowledge,

01:01:32.720 --> 01:01:35.750
and it's been great getting to
see more of the IT experience

01:01:35.750 --> 01:01:37.790
as well that we don't
always get to talk about

01:01:37.790 --> 01:01:38.780
in these hangouts.

01:01:38.780 --> 01:01:40.200
Thank you so much.

01:01:40.200 --> 01:01:41.190
My pleasure.

01:01:41.190 --> 01:01:43.510
Thank you for having me.

